# DE4SDV driving-state observer: SELinux template (NOT ACTIVE).
#
# This file is a skeleton, not policy. The `.te` extension is deliberately
# absent so a build never picks it up. Fill it only from a real denial captured
# on the IVI VM (sepolicy/README.md), and only for the domain the audit line
# names. Every type below is a placeholder: replace it, do not guess it.
#
# Where it goes when filled: the IVI image's BOARD_SEPOLICY_DIRS, as the AEBS
# ingress staged its own (a Soong `sepolicy` property is unsupported on this
# release):
#
#   vendor/de4sdv/driving_observer/sepolicy/de4sdv_driving_state_observer.te
#   vendor/de4sdv/driving_observer/sepolicy/board_sepolicy_driving.mk
#     BOARD_VENDOR_SEPOLICY_DIRS += vendor/de4sdv/driving_observer/sepolicy
#
# --- for a native fallback binary (the relay's shape), which is the case this
#     template is most likely to be used for: an init service with its own
#     domain, a gateway client, and the loopback line socket of the screen.
#
# type de4sdv_driving_state_observer, domain;
# type de4sdv_driving_state_observer_exec, exec_type, vendor_file_type, file_type;
# init_daemon_domain(de4sdv_driving_state_observer)
# sdv_gateway_client_domain(de4sdv_driving_state_observer)
#
# # The 127.0.0.1:4732 line socket for the observer app. Scoped to loopback and
# # the one port; no other network access.
# allow de4sdv_driving_state_observer self:tcp_socket { create bind listen accept read write shutdown getattr setopt };
# allow de4sdv_driving_state_observer port:tcp_socket { name_bind name_connect };
#
# --- for the app (only if the denial shows the app's domain lacks a gateway
#     allow that the image's own samples have):
#
# allow <app_domain> <gateway_service_type>:service_manager find;
# allow <app_domain> <gateway_service_type>:binder call;
# allow <app_domain> <gateway_socket_type>:unix_stream_socket connectto;
#
# Take the three type names above from the audit lines' `scontext`/`tcontext`
# fields. A denial whose `tcontext` is a `*_service` type without a
# `service_manager find` line usually means the app was not installed where the
# image expects it (sepolicy/README.md, fix 1).
